The workaround is to catch `NavigationStart` event and update ` CurrentNavigation` object
```javascript
export class AppComponent  {
  constructor(private _router: Router) {
    this._router.events.pipe(
        filter(e =>e instanceof NavigationStart),
        filter((e: NavigationStart)=>e.navigationTrigger == "popstate")
    )
      .subscribe((x: NavigationStart) => {
        this._router.getCurrentNavigation().extras.state={...x.restoredState, navigationId:x.id } ;
      });
  }
  }
```
Here is the StackBlitz example [https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-xyrmg1](https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-xyrmg1)  (Should be run in new window)

@jasonaden Are we sure this is a bug? I would argue that this is a new navigation triggered by the user, so why should the state be kept here?

In fact, for me this would be a problem. I am listening for changes to query parameters, but need to ignore these updates when the navigation has been triggered by a specific part of my code. I currently solve this by passing a flag via the state when I trigger the navigation.

If the user clicks the back button, this is now a user-triggered navigation, so passing the state would lead to an incorrect result.
